Refuse an update that writes over an earlier stretch or past the buffer.

After the screen turns, Gboard reopens the word before the caret and
then sends the text of that word as an update of a range it kept from
before: Chromium keeps the range of a composition where it was when the
page changes the text. In a line holding line two with the caret at its
end, two is written into 0..4 or into 7..8 with the caret left where it
was, and the line becomes two two or line twtwo; with line word it
becomes word word, and a longer remembered text grows it further
(twotwo, twotwowo). The key row's Tab inside a word does the mirror:
with the caret between WO and RD of WORD ONE the editor puts two spaces
there (WO  RD ONE, caret 4), the keyboard reopens and sends WO for 0..2,
which is that range's own text, and then RD, the word after the caret,
for 0..2 with the caret asked at 4, and the line becomes RD  RD ONE. A
keyboard that composes and is not Gboard does a third thing: with the
caret at the start of SEED BETA 1 and an a typed (0..0, caret 1) it
joins the letter to the word that follows and 423 to 518 ms later writes
aSEED over the letter (0..1, caret asked at 1), which gives aSEEDSEED
BETA 1. The editor applied each of these updates to the model. On an API
31 emulator with Gboard 10.8, 10 of 20 turns of the screen to landscape
and back, on a build without this patch, left the line damaged this way;
the Tab did it in 3 of 3 runs with Gboard 12.4 and in 2 of 3 on Gboard
10.8; the third form did it in 2 of 3 runs on HeliBoard 4.2.

A reopened word can also be written back short. With the caret inside the
word, Gboard 12.4 writes the part of the word up to the caret over the
whole range it kept and asks for the caret to stay: the text after the
caret that the range covers is dropped, and the keyboard goes on with the
same range, so its next update takes the characters after it as well. In a
line holding twzox with the caret between the z and the o it wrote twz
over 5..10 and the line became twz; in alpha beta Charlie delta with the
caret inside beta it wrote bet over 6..10, then bet over the same range
again, which took the space, and then betz over it, which put the z on the
capital.

On Android, while a composition is open and the selection is collapsed,
an update is refused when it asks for the caret to stay where it was,
replaces a range that is not empty and ends at or before the caret with
text that is not that range's own text, and the text it writes is
exactly a stretch of the buffer of its own length that contains the
caret or touches it: the stretch right before the caret, the one right
after it, or one that spans it. One kind of update that fits this is not
refused: a rewrite, with a text of the same length, of the range that
ends at the caret, in a composition that has already typed, that is, one
in which an update that changed the text was applied since its
compositionstart. A keyboard in step sends it when a Hangul syllable
gains a letter, when the word being composed grows in place or is
corrected (a kana or a multi-tap letter cycles, an autocorrect), and a
rule that refused it whenever the text after the caret holds the new
text refused it for a Korean keyboard each time the next characters were
the word being formed: on an API 36 emulator with Gboard 15.9 (Korean,
2-Bulsik), a line holding 가나 with the caret at its start, with the
consonant and then the vowel typed, sent ㄱ over 0..0 and then 가 over
0..1 with the caret kept, and the second update, refused, left the line
as ㄱ가나 where 가가나 was typed; the Japanese 12-key keyboard of the same
Gboard did the same when a kana was toggled (a line holding い with the
caret at its start, あ tapped and tapped again to い, sent あ over 0..0 and
then い over 0..1, and the refused second update left あい where いい was
typed, in 3 of 3 runs); the Korean keyboard rewrites the whole word so
far at each key, so a longer word typed in front of an identical one
meets the same refusal. Nor is one unit written over one unit: a kana
toggled by a 12-key keyboard on a reopened word, beside an identical
kana, is that, and no recorded stale write is (they are words, or one
unit over a word). A stale write comes as the first update of a
composition that only reopened a word, which has typed nothing, and it
stays refused. A refused update changes nothing in the model, with one
cursor or several: every cursor stays where it was. The input method's
buffer is written back to the model's text and the primary caret where
the update was applied to it, and input restarts, so the keyboard reads
the text and the caret again and does not go on with the range it kept.
Several refusals in one task restart input once, and nothing restarts if
the editor host no longer has the focus when the task's rewrites are
done. The rule keeps one flag, whether the composition that is open has
typed: it is cleared at compositionstart and set after the editor has
applied an update that changes the text (the model's version has moved)
or has typed a stale letter at the caret (below), and the exemption
above and the rule below read it. Apart from that, and from the range of
the last stale letter and the last replacement applied, which the stale
letters below read, an update is judged on what it says and on what the
editor holds when it arrives.

A second rule covers the tail drop. While a composition that has typed
nothing is open and the selection is collapsed, an update is refused when
it keeps the caret, its range starts before the caret and ends after it
inside the buffer, and its text is exactly the part of the range before
the caret and not the range's own text. A range whose own text is that
text repeated is passed: that is a keyboard taking a doubled word back
(twotwo to two). A composition that has typed is not judged by this rule,
as by the exemption above. Its refusal is the same as the other one:
nothing changes in the model, the buffer is written back, input restarts.

There is no third rule, and what was refused is counted, for the bound
below, and not remembered, but for the range of a stale letter typed at
the caret (below). An earlier form of this patch kept the last refusal,
with the model's version, the range it refused and the line its buffer
started at, and while that refusal stood it was to refuse the letter
typed next when it arrived over the same range (in alpha beta Charlie
delta, after the refused bet over 6..10, betz over 6..10 with the caret
asked at 10, which puts the z in place of the a). That rule could not
act. A refusal forces a restart in the microtask after the update, the
restart blurs the host, and the blur listener of 0046 clears the mark of
an open composition. Only compositionstart sets the mark again, and
compositionstart was also where the refusal ended. So with a new
composition the refusal was gone before the next update, and with none
the rule, which needs an open composition as the others do, did not
judge. It refused an update only when no restart had been made since the
refusal, which is two updates in one task: a script on the page can send
that, and a keyboard cannot, because the microtask runs when the
listener of the first update returns. The rule and its state are
removed, and the letter is applied in both cases, as it was. The rules
after it keep their numbers, fourth to ninth, so that the counts and the
device runs below read as they were written: the builds they call the
nine rules, and the ones they call this patch as it is, carried the
third as well. By the order of events above the removal changes the
outcome of no update that a keyboard sends, and on a device no update
came for the rule to judge: after the restart a refusal forces, Gboard
reads the text and the caret again, opens the word at the caret with the
word's own text and sends the next letter as an insertion at the caret.
It did not go on with the range that was refused in any run: the Tab
inside a word in 4 of 4 runs on an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and
in 3 of 3 on an API 31 emulator with Gboard 10.8 (RD over 0..2 refused,
RD opened again over 4..6, then z over 4..4, and the line read WO  zRD
ONE), and the tail drop sent to the editor while Gboard 12.4 held the
word open (bet over 6..10 refused, beta opened again over 6..10, then z
over 9..9), with the letter 0.4 to 3 s after the refusal.

What is refused is narrow by shape. A keyboard in step keeps the caret
on the same characters, so after an edit of the text before the caret it
asks for the caret at its old offset moved by the change in length, and
an update that does so is never refused by the first two rules when the
length changes: typing, Backspace, the autocorrect or completion of the
word before the caret, a suggestion, a conversion with the caret after
its text. An update that replaces a range is passed as well when it asks
for the caret moved by the change in length, as typing does: a keyboard
that sends the word with the letter just typed as the replacement of the
word's range (0..5, alphax, the caret asked at 6) is typing, at every
cursor, and the rule passes it for the caret it asks for and not for the
range it names. An update that changes the length before the caret and
asks for the caret to stay at its old offset is out of step by its own
shape; it is refused when its text is a stretch of the buffer that
contains or touches the caret, which is what the recorded writes are,
and a repeated syllable cannot be told from them. A rewrite of an
earlier stretch with a text of the same length keeps the caret by
construction, and it is refused only when its text is such a stretch and
differs from the range's own; when its range ends at the caret it is
passed as well in a composition that has typed. The first two rules
never refuse the same update: the first needs the range to end at or
before the caret, the second needs it to end after it.

Restarts are bounded by a count of the forced restarts that are made,
kept for one version of the model. It is one count for every rule and
every update: six restarts forced by different updates, under different
rules, spend it as six forced by the same update do. After six of them
without a change of text, each no more than half a minute after the one
before, the update that follows within that time is no longer refused:
it is applied as the editor without this patch applies it, so that a
keyboard that answers every restart with the same update, or a pick a
rule cannot tell from a stale write, is not refused for ever and the
person is not left with input that does nothing until something else is
typed. A stale letter (below) is the exception: typing its letter at the
caret does something, so it is typed past the bound as before it, and no
restart is forced for it there. The bound therefore does nothing for a
pick that has the shape of a stale letter: it is never applied, however
often it is tried, and each try types its letter after the word (below).
The bound does not ask whether the keyboard went on with the same
update: after six such restarts nothing more is refused, whatever those
six refused. The count starts again
after an update that changes the text has been applied by the editor,
that is when the text of the update differs from the cached text of its
range and the model's version is not the one it had before the edit, and
after any other edit of the model (Enter by beforeinput, a paste, a cut,
an undo), because the count belongs to the version of the model it was
made on. It starts again too when an update is judged more than half a
minute after the last counted restart, by the page's own clock
(performance.now), which does not step when the phone's time is set.
That wait is what keeps the refusals of separate moments, a turn of the
screen now and another a minute later with nothing typed between them,
from adding up to six, and neither party the bound is for is slowed by
it: a keyboard that answers a restart with the same update does so
within a second (283 to 324 ms apart on a device), and a person who
tries a refused pick again does so within seconds. The count does not
start again at a touch on the page or at a turn of the screen. It did
for a while, and a person whose pick was refused, and who tapped the
word between tries, then never reached the seventh. On an API 33
emulator with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134, 36 round trips of the screen
to landscape and back with the word two open at the end of line two gave
one stale write that was refused (two over 0..4, the caret kept at 8),
and 48 taps inside words gave none; a count that only a change of text
started again would have kept that one for good. On the same emulator a
stale write made by hand (a TextUpdateEvent on the edit context) was
refused six times and applied the seventh with a tap in the editor
before each, as it was with none; after five of them and 34 s with no
forced restart the count was dropped at the next (five restarts, 34,084
ms since the last), six were refused and the seventh applied. On an
earlier build of this patch, which read the wall clock, 8 s in place of
that wait left the count standing: the sixth was refused and the seventh
applied. A restart counts where its blur and focus happen: refusals
handed over in one task make one restart and count once, and a refusal
while the editor host does not have the focus makes none and counts for
nothing. Inside that half minute nothing else starts the count again:
not an update that the editor discards (a read-only editor, a cursor
that loses its edit), not one that names a range with its own text, a
selection that moves (the keyboard makes those too), an empty update, a
new composition or a change of focus; the restarts the editor makes
after a rewrite of the buffer are not counted. A keyboard that is
refused twice, once for the range of the word and once for the range of
its last letter, as it was in three of 40 runs on a device (283 to 324
ms apart), is well inside it. What it costs: the update that follows six
forced restarts on one version of the model, each no more than half a
minute after the one before, and comes within half a minute of the
sixth, is applied, whatever its shape but that of a stale letter, and
the letter or the word it carries may do what the stale write does; and
a pick the rules took for a stale write, tried again more than half a
minute apart each time, is refused each time. The member, the version it
belongs to, the time of the last counted restart, the guard at the
refusal and at its restart, the count in the restart and the resets are
marked in the source as the parts of the bound and go together.

What it costs. A correction of an earlier word that keeps the caret in
place and produces a text that the buffer holds in a stretch that
contains or touches the caret is refused and input restarts; the typo
stays. The first case is the very word that stands before the caret (teh
the, the first word corrected to the with the caret at the end); the
mirror is the very word that follows the caret (teh the with the caret
between the space and the); and a stretch that spans the caret is the
third (xabx, ab written over the x with the caret between the a and the
b). A repeated syllable is refused too: in haha with the caret at the
end, the last a replaced by ha with the caret asked at 4 cannot be told
from the recorded stale writes. So is the first update of a composition
that only reopened a word when it rewrites, with the same length, the
range that ends at the caret and the text after the caret holds the new
text (a suggestion tapped on a reopened word): it cannot be told from
the Tab. The second rule refuses, with the tail drops, what has the same
shape: a take-back that drops part of a repeated word (twotwowo with the
caret at 6, twotwo written over 0..8), and the deletion of the rest of a
word sent as a rewrite of the whole word (hello world with the caret at
2, he written over 0..5). A text that the buffer holds only away from
the caret passes. Not recognised: a write of the remembered text with
the caret moved by the change in length, one whose text is not such a
stretch, one written when the remembered text no longer stands there, a
stale write that is a same-length rewrite of the range that ends at the
caret and arrives in a composition that had already typed (it has the
shape of a word that grows in place, which the Korean and Japanese
keyboards above send in step, so it is passed), a tail drop that arrives
in a composition that has typed, the update that a keyboard sends for
the next typed letter if it still holds its old range (range 0..4, text
twox, caret asked at 4), a range that reaches past the caret with a text
that is not the part before it (the shape of a brace typed inside a
word, which another change handles), the letter typed arriving as the
replacement of the whole word (line x), and a word sent again as an
insertion at the caret (HeliBoard at a turn). Nor is the letter typed
over a stale range: the part of the word up to the caret and the letter,
written over the whole range with the caret asked right after the letter
(betz over 6..10), is applied whether or not a refusal came before it,
so the letter lands and the tail is lost (alpha betz Charlie delta). A
suggestion taken with the caret inside a word has the same shape (alpha
with the caret after al, all written over 0..5 with the caret at 3), so
the shape alone cannot be refused. The restart is the keyboard's blur
and focus, so it ends the open composition each time, and until a
composition starts again only the refusal of a range that ends beyond
the buffer, and the range of a stale letter typed at the caret (below),
judge an update: a stale write that the keyboard sends again with no
composition open is applied.

A reopened word can also be written over by one letter. After the screen
turns, Gboard 12.4 reopens the word at the caret (the word before it, or
the one it is in) with its own text and nothing typed; the first letter
typed is then written over the whole range it reopened and asks for the
caret right after it: in a line holding line two with the caret at its
end, x over 5..8 with the caret asked at 6, and the line becomes line x;
in a line holding alpha beta Charlie delta with the caret between the l
and the t of delta, z over 19..24 with the caret asked at 20, and the
line becomes alpha beta Charlie z. Of the 33,690 recorded updates of
3,286 recordings, 32
have this shape, one in each of 32 runs; 10 of them are the tail drop of
the second rule as well, which refuses it too (the letter is the first
unit of the range and the caret is right after it). Of the other 22, 17
are in runs that ended with the word gone or cut, 3 in runs that ended
intact, where the build refused the letter and it was typed again, and 2
in runs without a record of how they ended. No Korean or Japanese update
has the shape. On Android, while a composition is open that has typed
nothing since its compositionstart and the selection is collapsed, an
update is refused when it writes one letter or digit of an alphabet over
a range that is longer than that unit and that the caret is inside or at
the end of, and asks for the caret right after it. One unit is one code
point: a letter outside the basic plane is two units long and is judged
as one. The unit is not refused when it is the first unit of a range of
two units, which is what a Backspace on a reopened two-letter word
writes (a longer word that starts with the unit is refused). A selected
word typed over has a selection that is not collapsed and is not
touched. A unit of Han, kana or Hangul stands for a longer reading, and
a symbol is not what the stale writes were: one kanji for two kana, or a
symbol or an emoji from the suggestions, is not judged; none of the 32
recorded updates is one.

Two rules that stood here, the fifth and the sixth, are removed, and the
rules keep their numbers. The fifth refused a remembered word written
over text that lies after the caret with the caret asked to stay: after
the screen turns, Gboard 12.4 on an API 34 emulator reopened be over
6..8 of alpha beta Charlie delta with the caret at 2 and then wrote
alpha over 6..8 with the caret asked at 2, and the line became alpha
alphata Charlie delta. The sixth refused the range's own text written
back with units added at its end, the caret kept at the range's end:
Gboard 12.4 on an API 33 emulator wrote twox over 44..47, which held
two, with the caret asked at 47, and the line became line twox where
nothing was typed. Each asks for the caret to stay at an offset that is
not the end of what it writes, in a composition that has typed nothing,
with a range that is not empty, lies in the buffer and does not hold the
text written, which is the shape the seventh rule below refuses, so the
seventh refuses every update either of them refused. Of the 33,690
recorded updates of 3,286 recordings, 2 have the fifth rule's shape and
89 the sixth's.

A write that nobody typed can also keep the caret where it was while it
replaces text, wherever the text comes from. After the screen turns,
Gboard 12.4 on an API 33 emulator reopened the word at the end of the
last line of a buffer holding line twox and line two with its own text
(two over 15..18, the caret at 18) and, in the same composition and with
nothing typed in it, wrote line over the same range with the caret asked
at 18: not after the new text, which is at 19, and not where a keyboard
in step asks for it after a change of length. The line became line line,
and the letter typed next gave line linxe; 2 of 20 turns of the screen
to landscape and back, on a build with the rules above, left the line
damaged this way. No rule above sees it: the text it writes, line, is
not a stretch of the buffer that touches the caret. The text of such a
write is the range's text at another time, so what it says does not tell
it from a rewrite; where it puts the caret does. On Android, while a
composition is open that has typed nothing since its compositionstart
and the selection is collapsed before and in the update, an update is
refused when it asks for the caret to stay at the offset it had, the
range it replaces is not empty and inside the buffer, its text is not
the range's own, and that offset is not the end of what it writes. The
rule keeps no state and reads nothing but the update, the buffer and the
flag that the other rules read. Of the 33,690 recorded updates of 3,286
recordings, 435 have the shape (2 of them write the empty text), in 387
runs, and every one asks for the caret at the offset it had. 427 were
refused by the rules before it when the count was made, the fifth and
sixth among them, 266 of them in runs that ended intact. 8 are not: the
one above and a second of the same shape, two writes of a word as
nothing (two over 5..8 as the empty text, the caret kept at 8, which
left line x), a doubled word cut back (wotwotwo over 6..14 written as
two, the caret kept at 8, which left line ttwxo), a space written over
ana in the first line of a buffer (63..66, the caret kept at 75), which
the build refused, and two writes over the first word of a buffer on a
build without these rules, which left WORD ONE as RDD ONE and as
RDNEEsecondecond. 5 of the 8 are in runs that ended with the text
damaged, one in a run that ended intact, where the build refused it, and
2 in runs without a record of how they ended; none was applied in a run
that ended intact. No Korean or Japanese update has the shape: the 1,729
recorded updates of Korean, Japanese or Chinese text that replace a
range in an open composition all ask for the caret at the end of what
they write, and all are on a composition that has typed. A keyboard that
corrects an earlier word asks for the caret moved by the change in
length, and a suggestion or a correction taken at the word asks for it
after the text; neither is refused.

What the seventh rule costs. A correction of an earlier word that keeps
the caret at the offset it had, with the caret not at the end of what it
writes, is refused and input restarts: the typo stays. The refusal does
not depend on the text, so it widens the first cost above. No recorded
keyboard sent one; one that corrects a word of the same length and asks
for the caret where it was would (the over 0..3 of teh quick with the
caret asked at 9). The same update sent again is refused until six
restarts have been made without a change of text, and the seventh is
applied. Not recognised: a stale write that asks for the caret at the
end of what it writes, or moved by the change in length, which is what a
suggestion taken asks for (lin over 5..8 of line two with the caret
asked at 8 gives line lin).

A reopened word can also be written again at the caret as an insertion.
After the screen turns, Gboard 12.4 reopens the word before the caret
with an update that changes nothing (an empty range at the caret, no
text, the caret asked to stay), and, a second or so later and with
nothing typed in the composition, writes that word again as an insertion
at the caret, asking for the caret at the offset it had: before the text
it inserts. In a line holding line two with the caret at its end, two
inserted at 8..8 with the caret asked at 8, and the line becomes line
twotwo; the letter typed next gives line twoxtwo, on the screen and on
the disk. No rule above sees it: every rule above needs a range that is
not empty. The text of such an insertion is what the buffer already
holds before the caret, so what it says does not tell it from a word
that is new; where it puts the caret does, because every other insertion
at the caret in the recordings asks for the caret after its text. On
Android, while a composition is open that has typed nothing since its
compositionstart and the selection is collapsed before and in the
update, an update is refused when its range is empty, at the caret and
inside the buffer, its text is not empty, and it asks for the caret at
the offset it had. The rule keeps no state and reads nothing but the
update, the buffer and the flag that the other rules read. Of the 33,690
recorded updates of 3,286 recordings, 74 insert text at the caret and
ask for the caret before it; 73 of them are in a composition that has
typed nothing, and all 73 write again the text that stands right before
the caret, after an update with an empty range at the same place, 200 ms
to 5.1 s earlier (72 of those updates opened the composition). 58 are in
runs that ended with the text damaged, 4 in runs that ended intact (3
where the keyboard took the doubled word back itself some time later, 1
that the build refused), and 11 in runs without a record of how they
ended. The 5,384 updates that insert at the caret in such a composition
and ask for the caret after the text, as a typed letter or a suggestion
taken does, are not refused. None of the 73 is Korean or Japanese text.

What the eighth rule costs. An insertion at the caret that asks for the
caret to stay before the text it inserts is refused and input restarts:
a completion shown after the caret by a keyboard that asks for the caret
to stay before it would be lost. No recorded keyboard sent one. The same
update sent again is refused until six restarts have been made without a
change of text, and the seventh is applied. Not recognised: the same
insertion in a composition that has typed (one is recorded, in a run
that ended damaged), an insertion elsewhere than at the caret that keeps
the caret (five are recorded: the doubling at the end of the line in two
runs that ended damaged, two typed passages whose insertion ends at the
caret, and a text written again with the caret after it), and an
insertion that asks for the caret after the text, which a typed letter
and a suggestion taken ask for.

A reopened word can also be written over the letter just typed. After
the screen turns and a letter is typed at the end of a line, Gboard 10.8
on an API 31 emulator marks the word before the caret again and writes a
text over the stretch that ends at the caret, where the letter stands,
asking for the caret to stay at the offset it had. Nearly always the
text is the word with the letter (twox over the x, twotwox), which the
first rule takes: the text is a stretch of the buffer that touches the
caret. Twice in the recordings it was a text from elsewhere in the
buffer. With a first line ending in haha no no banana above line two and
an x typed at the end of line two, a space and no b, a stretch of the
first line (56..61), were written over the x (75..76) with the caret
asked at 76, where it was, and the line became line two no b: the letter
was lost. The first rule reads what the text says and cannot tell a text
that is held away from the caret; where the update puts the caret does.
A keyboard in step asks for the caret at the end of what it writes, as a
typed letter, a Backspace, a correction on Space, a Hangul or kana step
and a conversion each do. On Android, while a composition is open that
has typed and the selection is collapsed before and in the update, an
update is refused when its range is not empty, ends at the caret and
lies in the buffer, its text is not empty, and it asks for the caret at
the offset it had, which is not the end of what it writes (the start of
the range plus the length of the text). An update that writes the
range's own text has the end of the range for the end of what it writes
and is never refused. The rule keeps no state and reads nothing but the
update, the buffer and the two flags that the other rules read, and its
refusal is the same as theirs. Of the 33,690 recorded updates of 3,286
recordings, 223 have this shape, one in each of 223 runs. 221 write a
text that touches the caret, which the first rule refuses as well; the
other 2 are the two above, in runs that ended damaged. 184 of the 223
are in runs that ended intact, where the build the run was made on
refused each one; none was applied in a run that ended intact. None is
Korean or Japanese text.

What the ninth rule costs. An update that replaces the text that ends at
the caret and keeps the caret at the offset it had, which is not the end
of what it writes, is refused and input restarts once. A completion
written over the letter just typed with a longer text, by a keyboard
that keeps the caret at the letter's offset, would be lost and the
letter would stay; no recorded keyboard outside the 223 sent one. The
same update sent again is refused until six restarts have been made
without a change of text, and the seventh is applied. Not recognised: a
range that ends before the caret (23 are recorded, all refused by the
first rule) or after it (14 are recorded, 8 in runs that ended damaged
and 6 in runs without a record of how they ended), an empty text, and
the same update in a composition that has typed nothing, which the
seventh rule takes.

A write whose range ends beyond the buffer can also arrive. The input
method keeps the offsets of the buffer it was given, so when its copy is
longer than the buffer the editor holds, or the editor has put the start
of the buffer elsewhere since, a range it kept from before names text
that the buffer does not hold. On a test build that typed the letter
itself and then rebuilt the buffer at the caret's line, after the screen
turned and a letter was typed, Gboard 10.8 on an API 31 emulator wrote
the word over 74..75 and asked for the caret at 9, in a buffer of nine
units that started 67 units after the start the keyboard knew. The
editor read the 66 units after the caret as that many units to replace,
and the typed edit of a composition replaces them in the model across
line breaks (patch 0024): the line break and the line below the caret
were deleted and the word stood twice (line twoxtwox). Such an update is
now refused in any composition state: the model keeps its text, the
buffer goes back to it, input restarts once, and the refusal counts in
the bound of restarts. Past the bound it is passed as every refusal is
passed, but its range is cut at the buffer first: the end becomes the
end of the buffer and the start is limited to it, so that the typed edit
replaces no unit after the caret that the buffer does not hold and a
deletion that starts beyond the buffer deletes nothing. The cut copies
the five fields of the update one by one, because the browser's update
event keeps them as getters on its prototype and a copy made with a
spread would hold none of them. The text after the buffer is not
touched. A range that ends at the end of the buffer is a write over its
last units and is not beyond it. Of the 38,774 recorded updates of 3,733
recordings, 40 end beyond the buffer that the editor had written last, 5
of them start beyond it: 35 in recordings made without this patch or
with an earlier form of it, none with the later forms, and 5 on the
test build above, each in a run that ended with
the line below the caret deleted: the line read line twoxtwox in 4 and
line twoxx in 1. They lie in 24 runs: 14 ended with the text damaged, 10
have no record of how they ended, and none ended intact. None of the
2,660 recorded updates outside a composition ends beyond the buffer, and
443 of them replace a range, a word replaced by a suggestion or a
correction among them, inside it. What it costs: an input method that is
one unit ahead of the buffer is refused where it was applied. 15 of the
40 updates are such (13 in recordings made without this patch, 2 with
an earlier form of it), none on the later forms.

The letter typed after the screen turns. A keyboard that reopened the
word at the caret goes on from a composition it kept from before the
turn, so the next letter arrives as a write into that composition and
not at the caret. Three such writes were recorded: the letter alone over
the whole reopened word (x over 5..8, the caret asked at 6, the fourth
rule's shape, which the rule refused and which lost the letter), on
Gboard 10.8 on an API 31 emulator and, in the recordings above, on
Gboard 12.4; the letter over the last letter of the word (x over 7..8,
the caret kept at 8, which no rule refused and which left line twx where
line twox was typed), on Gboard 10.8; and an earlier word with the same
text, grown by the letter (toe over 4..6, the caret asked at 7, in a
line holding tob to tod to with the caret at its end, which put the
letter into the earlier word and moved the caret into it), on Gboard
10.8 and on Gboard 12.4 on an API 33 emulator (WebView 134 on both
emulators). In each the range and the text are those of the old
composition and the letter is the only new part, typed at the caret by
the person. An update of one of these shapes is now refused as before,
the letter is typed at the caret as one edit, and input restarts, so the
keyboard reads line twox and the caret after the x.

The last two shapes are told from honest updates by what they say. The
letter over the last letter has the caret kept, a range of one unit that
ends at the caret, a composition that has typed nothing, and two letters
of one code point each whose base letters differ, case aside, a base
letter being what is left of a letter without the accents that NFD takes
off, and both base letters being of the basic Latin alphabet. A letter
over itself in the other case or with an accent (é over e, or â over a,
which a Vietnamese Telex aa writes) has the same base letter, and a
keyboard that cycles a letter does it in a composition that has typed.
No other letter is judged, because a step on a reopened letter would
have this shape where its keyboard writes it, though no recording here
holds such a step, nor a stale letter of those scripts: a letter with a
stroke, which NFD does not take apart (đ over d, which a Telex dd
writes), a transliteration (a Devanagari ka picked over k), a phonetic
or multi-tap step (a Bengali kha over ka, a Cyrillic be over a), a final
form (an Arabic teh marbuta over heh), a Turkish dotless i over i. Han,
kana and Hangul are left out with them, as the fourth rule leaves them
out, and digits are not letters. What it costs: a stale letter of those
scripts written over the last letter still replaces it, since nothing it
says tells it from such a step. A last letter outside the basic plane is
two units and is not judged. An earlier form of this rule took only the
basic Latin alphabet without accents, so an é written over a different
last letter still replaced it (line twé). The earlier word's range ends
before the caret without touching it, its text is the range's own text
and one letter more, and the caret is asked right after the written
text: a correction of the word before a space asks for the caret to move
by the change in length, and the word under the caret touches it. Nor is
the caret asked where it is: a keyboard that commits the word before a
single space asks for that, because Chromium puts the caret at the end
of a commit (then over the of the and a space, the caret asked at 4),
which an earlier form of this rule took for the stale write, typing the
n after the space; the stale write moved it back (7 against 13). What it
costs: a stale growth of the word right before a single space asks for
the caret where it is as well, and goes into that word (to to and a
space with the caret after it, toe over 3..5 with the caret asked at 6,
gives to toe and a space), where that earlier form typed the letter at
the caret (to to e). The count of forced restarts goes on across the
letters typed this way, and after six in a row, with nothing else typed
and less than half a minute between them, the letter is still typed at
the caret and no restart is forced for it: a keyboard that answers every
restart with the same stale write is not asked to read the text again,
and the replay ends. What it costs: such a keyboard has the letter of
one key typed seven times (x over 5..8 sent after every restart gives
line twoxxxxxxx); no recorded keyboard replays a stale write so. An
earlier form of this patch applied the seventh write as it came, which
put the letter alone over the word (line x). The letter typed is the one
the keyboard wrote. In 5 of the 645 runs of the batches below it was not
the key that was tapped, 3 of them in plain typing, which no rule here
sees.

What the stale letters cost. A pick of one letter from the suggestion
strip over a word that the keyboard reopened, with nothing typed in that
composition, has the shape of a stale letter, and nothing it says tells
it from one. Over a word of more letters it is the letter alone over the
word: is, a space and a Backspace, then I picked over is, gives isI, and
d picked over cl gives cld. Over a word of one letter it is the letter
over the last letter when both are letters of the basic Latin alphabet,
accented or not, with different base letters: c picked over x gives xc,
and é picked over a gives aé, which the earlier form of that rule, which
took no accented letter, applied. The pick is typed after the word as a
stale letter is, and trying again does not help: each try has the same
shape over the word as it has grown, its letter is typed after it again,
and past the bound of restarts it is still typed, with no restart forced
(seven tries of I over is give isIIIIIII). Applied as before are the
word's own first letter picked over a word of two letters, which is a
Backspace's shape, a pick over a word of one letter that differs from it
by case or accents alone or is not of the basic Latin alphabet, and a
pick of Han, kana, Hangul or a symbol. The person sees the letter after
the word at once and the word stays as it was.

A keyboard that did not read the text again after its stale letter was
typed goes on with the range it kept. In a line holding line two, x over
5..8 is typed at the caret and the line reads line twox, and the next
letter, y, arrives over 5..8 again, with a new composition or with none;
it was applied as it came and wrote line yx where line twoxy was typed.
None of the shapes above sees it there: the caret is past the range now,
and no rule but the guard judges an update with no composition open. The
range of the update whose letter was typed is kept with the version of
the model and the primary selection that the letter left, and while the
model is at that version and the caret is where the letter left it, an
update of the same range whose text is one letter, or the range's own
text and one letter, and that asks for the caret right after what it
writes, as the stale letter did, is the next letter sent the same way
and is typed at the caret too, with a composition open or none, as a
stale letter is. A keyboard that read the text again names the caret and
the word at it, not that range, and any edit of the model moves the
version. A caret that is elsewhere was told to the keyboard, and the
same offsets then name another place: after the stale toe over 4..6 in
tob to tod to, a tap at the end of the earlier to and a Backspace send t
over 4..6, a deletion of the o there, and on the next line, once the
buffer starts at the caret's line, i over 4..6 deletes the f of if. So
the update is judged on its own while the caret is elsewhere. The caret
is compared, not followed: moved away and back with no edit between, it
reads the range again. An update of the range that asks for the caret
anywhere but right after what it writes is judged on its own as well: a
keyboard that corrects the earlier to of that line to top asks for the
caret moved by the change in length (top over 4..6, the caret asked at
15), and the correction is applied (tob top tod toe), where a test of
the range and the text alone would type the p at the caret (tob to tod
toep). Nor is the first letter of a word of two letters written over
both taken for the next letter: it is a Backspace, which the fourth rule
passes as well, and it is judged on its own. It is told by the text the
range held when the letter was typed and by the text the range holds
now, which differ once the letter's cursor event starts the buffer at
the caret's line: with xx to above zz a, the stale toe over 3..5 is
typed as zz ae, and a Backspace on ae sends a over 3..5, which a test of
the text the range held alone would take for the next letter (zz aea).
What it costs: a next letter sent over the old offsets whose text starts
a text of two letters that the buffer holds there now is taken for that
Backspace and judged on its own. The range's own text is kept with it as
the buffer held it when the letter was typed: the letter's own cursor
event can start the buffer at the caret's line, and the keyboard goes on
with the offsets of the buffer before, so with line one above tob to tod
to, the grown toe over 13..15 and then tof over 13..15 name text that
the buffer from the caret's line no longer holds there. More than one
letter over the range, or a combining mark alone, is judged on its own,
as before the range was kept (xy over 5..8 after the x gives line xyx).
No recording holds a Backspace or a correction sent over that range
either. Neither Gboard recorded did this: after every restart recorded
the keyboard read the text again.

A keyboard can also take back a correction that the editor applied for
it, and the take-back has the shape of one letter over a reopened word:
u corrected to you and a space, the space deleted with a Backspace, and
you reopened and written back as u over 0..3 with the caret asked at 1,
which typed the u after the word (youu). The last update the editor
applied that changed the text is kept with the version of the model it
left, where the buffer started once it was applied, where the update
starts, the text it replaced and the text it wrote. A deletion at the
end of the text it wrote, which is what the Backspace on the space
sends, keeps it with that text cut, and an insertion at that end of a
text with no letter, digit or mark in it keeps it with that text added:
a keyboard that sends the correction and its space as two updates (you
over 0..1, then a space at 3..3) sends the space so, and before the
space was kept the take-back after its Backspace typed the u after the
word as well. A letter, a digit or a mark typed there goes on with the
word and is kept as an update of its own. An update that writes the
replaced text back, from where that update started, over the text it
wrote (the buffer holds that text there), with the model at that version
and the buffer starting where it did, is not a stale letter: it is
judged by the other rules, which apply it. The buffer's start is part of
the test because a tap or a cursor key that starts the buffer at another
line moves no version, and the same offsets then name that line: with
you on the next line too, a tap at its end and u over 0..3 there would
be taken for the take-back and the line would become u, its yo lost;
with the start tested, the u is typed after that you (youu), as a stale
letter is. The start is compared, not followed: moved to another line
and back with no edit between, the buffer starts where it did and the
take-back is applied. A text that starts with what it replaced is a word
completed or grown as it is typed (t to the), not a correction, and the
first letter written over it after a turn stays the stale letter it
looks like. Anything else that edits the model ends what is kept, a
Backspace that the editor runs as a key press among them (Gboard 17.7
and 18.4 send it so): that edit is the editor's own and no update
reports it, so the take-back after it is typed as a stale letter (youu),
as it was before anything was kept. What it costs: the same letter typed
after a turn that follows such a correction, with nothing typed between
but a space or a punctuation mark at the end of the correction or a
deletion there, cannot be told from the take-back and is applied (u
typed after a turn that follows u corrected to you gives u). Not run on
a device: no recording holds a take-back.

What the patch does not do. A letter that the keyboard never sends is
not typed: no update carried it in 3 of 212 runs without this patch and
in 2 of 103 with it, on Gboard 10.8, and in 4 of 40 runs on Gboard 12.4
while a second emulator ran on the host, and in none of the 70 runs on a
quiet host, with the patch or without it. The editor has nothing to type
then. A stale write of another shape is judged by the rules above, as
before.

Nor does the patch remember what it refused, but for the range of a
stale letter typed at the caret (above). The restart ends the
composition, and the update that comes next is judged on its own: if the
keyboard goes on with the range it kept and sends the next letter over
it (betz over 6..10 above), the letter is applied, with a new
composition open or with none. Whether a restarted keyboard still sends
that update, and whether a compositionstart comes before it, is not
recorded here.

The source checks described below were made on forms of this patch that
carried the third rule, with a harness that is not in the repository.
What the repository runs is scripts/check-editcontext-ime.js, which
build-vscode-oss.sh runs on the patched tree: named cases over this file
as the series leaves it, among them each rule here, the bound, the
refusal of a range beyond the buffer, the letter after a refusal
replayed with a composition that starts again and with none, the stale
letters, the take-back of a correction and the picks the stale letters
cost. It runs the real emit method over the real composition and replace
operations, with the model, the cursor list and the EditContext replaced
by fakes and no undo stack, on a host Node and not the Android runtime,
and its update events are objects whose fields are getters on a
prototype, as the browser's are.

The harness outside the repository ran the same method with the model,
the cursor list, the undo stack and the EditContext replaced by
adapters. Its named cases covered the recorded damage, the device rows
above, the Tab and the joined letter, the tail
drops, what the rules cost, the restart, the bound, the refusal that
stands, the cases the rules cannot tell from a stale write, which are
kept as known limits and say what the patch does, for the seventh rule,
the two updates that damaged runs replayed from their recordings, the
five other recorded updates that the rules above passed, each kind of
update that must still be applied, and each condition of the rule alone,
for the eighth rule, the update that damaged a run replayed from its
recording with the letter typed after it, each recorded update of its
shape, each kind of insertion that must still be applied (a letter typed
at the caret, a suggestion with the caret after it, an emoji, a text
with no composition open, a composition that starts with an update that
changes nothing) and each condition of the rule alone, and, for the
ninth rule, the two updates that damaged runs replayed from their
recordings, each of the 18 states in which a recorded update of its
shape was sent, each kind of update that must still be applied (a second
letter, a Backspace, a correction on Space, a jamo step, a kana step, a
conversion, a suggestion taken) and each condition of the rule alone,
and, for the guard, the stale write of the device run in the buffer it
arrived in with a composition open, with one that has typed and with
none open, the units it would replace after the caret reaching the end
of the file, a range one unit beyond the buffer, and, which are not
beyond it, a range that ends exactly at the end of the buffer and a
write over the last line of a multi-line buffer, the bound, a deletion
that starts beyond the buffer, with one cursor and with two, a text
written over a range that starts beyond it, a platform that is not
Android, and each action that rebuilds the buffer at the caret's line
while a composition is open (a tap, an arrow key, a paste). The guard is
checked against its statement on every buffer of up to five units, every
range from before the buffer to three units past it, every text, every
caret, composing or not and typed or not, with two lines after the
buffer's: it refuses exactly the updates that end beyond the buffer, and
past the bound it passes them clamped with the line break and the lines
after the buffer untouched, the line of the buffer made of the text
before the range, the text of the update and what follows the buffer,
the caret, for an update with a text, at the offset it asks for, and the
outcome that of the update that ends at the end of the buffer, with one
cursor and with two (49,522 evaluations, none disagrees). 116 deliberate
breakages of the code, one literal edit each (96 of the earlier rules
and 20 of the guard and its clamp): a case or a check of a method
against its statement fails on 113 of them. The 3 that survive are the
two that remove the call of the fifth rule or of the sixth, whose
refusals the seventh rule contains, and the one that removes the
condition on the end of the buffer from the sixth rule, which the guard,
first in the chain, now holds for every rule. The fifth and sixth rules
have since been removed for that reason. 10,466,336 small updates agree
with the fourth rule written separately from the code, 6,239,744 with
the fifth rule and the sixth rule, 10,126,208 with the seventh,
15,468,864 with the eighth and 15,468,864 with the ninth. Over the
33,690 recorded updates of 3,286 recordings the patch differs from the
version before it on 2 updates. Over the 38,774 recorded updates of
3,733 recordings it differs from the same patch without the guard on 59
updates: the 40 above, and 19 that end beyond the replay's copy of the
buffer, which is one to six units shorter than the buffer the editor had
written, and not beyond the editor's.

On devices. On an API 31 emulator with Gboard 10.8 and WebView 134, a
word was typed, the screen turned to landscape and back, and one letter
typed about eight seconds later. Without this patch 21 of 30 runs ended
damaged, 20 of them with the word doubled, on the disk too. With the
nine rules built, 100 of 101 runs were right: 39 of 40 on a new line and
40 of 40 on a line reached by a tap, both under a load held on the host,
and 21 of 21 on a quiet host, where the keyboard had the word open at
the letter in 20. The ninth rule's shape came in 31 of those runs and
was refused in each. The run that was not right ended line twx, the
letter over the last letter, as said above.

The write beyond the buffer was dispatched into the live editor through
the page, after the turn and with the keyboard attached: twox over one
unit that lies 65 units past the caret, as recorded, and x over a range
that starts before the caret and ends 20 units past the buffer, each
with a composition open that had typed nothing, with one that had typed,
and with none open. On a build with the nine rules and without the
refusal the lines after the caret were deleted on the disk in 12 runs of
12. With the patch built the whole file was kept in 30 runs of 30, one
restart for the refused update and the next two letters at the caret;
the recorder shows a composition open at the update in 19 of the 35 runs
of that batch and none in 16. The same update sent again and again was
refused until six refusals stood for one version of the model, then
passed once with its range cut: its text was typed at the end of the
buffer, the lines after it were whole, and nothing read undefined, in 5
runs of 5. An empty text over a range beyond the buffer, sent seven
times with the caret inside the word, changed nothing in 6 runs of 6,
and in the 3 of them where an Undo followed it took back the typed line,
so the passed update had left no undo step.

On an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134, the refusal held
in 15 runs of 15 in the three states, the composition open and untyped
at the update in all five runs of that state. The bound held in 7 of 7:
six refusals, the seventh update passed with its range cut at the end of
the buffer, the eighth refused again. Past the bound an empty text
wholly beyond the buffer changed nothing and one Undo took back the
letter typed before it in 3 of 3, and an empty text from two units after
the caret to five past the buffer deleted to the end of the buffer and
nothing after it in 3 of 3.

With the patch as it is here, the turn and the letter were run 28 times
more on the API 31 emulator, with no load made on the host: 14 of 14 on
a new line came out right, with two more letters typed after the first,
and 12 of 14 on a line reached by a tap. In the two that were not, the
keyboard wrote the letter alone over the whole reopened word, which is
refused, and the letter was lost, as said above. No update of the 28
runs ended beyond the buffer, and the patch without the refusal of such
a range decides every one of their 485 updates the same. On the API 33
emulator with Gboard 12.4 the turn and a letter were right in 10 runs of
10 with it, the keyboard writing the word and the letter together each
time.

With the letter typed at the caret, the turn and one letter, typed 0.4,
0.7, 1.0 and 1.5 s after the keyboard was back, were run on a fresh word
of two letters, on the published server and on this patch (one
workbench.js swapped into the same debug build); the batches with an
earlier word and with the word alone cleared the line before each run,
the batches on a line that grew did not. Gboard 10.8, API 31: with an
earlier word of the same text in front of the new one (to to), 8 of 50
runs without the patch put the letter into the earlier word and none of
40 with it, the stale write coming in 5 of those 40 and typed at the
caret each time; with the word alone, 0 of 42 and 0 of 30. On a line
that grew by a word at every run, 8 of 120 runs without the patch lost
the letter or damaged the line (the letter over the whole word in 2,
over the last letter in 1, an earlier word in 2, a letter never sent in
1, and 2 after the line was already damaged) and none of 33 with it was
damaged (one letter was never sent), the three shapes coming once each
and typed at the caret. Gboard 12.4, API 33: with the earlier word, 4 of
40 runs without the patch put the letter into it; with the patch none of
40 on a quiet host (no stale write came in that batch) and none of 40 on
a host running a second emulator, where the stale write came in 7 and
was typed at the caret; with the word alone, 0 of 30 without it and 0 of
60 with it.

The honest neighbours, on the build with this patch and the one after it
(0054): five runs each of seven sequences, with the line cleared before
each, on Gboard 12.4, API 33, and on Gboard 10.8, API 31. A sentence
typed (hello world); a Backspace at the end of a word; two Backspaces; a
letter typed inside a word after the caret was moved with two Left keys
(helxlo); a word typed, the screen turned and two letters typed
(wordst); and, which tested nothing, teh and Space, and i and Space,
because the keyboard offered no correction in the editor and the text
stayed as typed. All 70 runs ended with the text expected. Not run on a
device: a one-letter pick from the strip over a reopened word, a
correction on Space that the keyboard makes, a Korean or a Japanese
keyboard.

Other keyboards, on the nine rules: on the API 33 emulator with Gboard
12.4, which composes English words, twenty runs of the turn and a letter
were right, and fifteen runs of the rules' honest neighbours were right
with no refusal (letters typed and deleted at a word's end, a word taken
from the strip, a letter typed inside a word); in five more a word typed
for correction stayed as typed, because the keyboard offered no
correction in the editor; on an API 36 emulator with Gboard 18.5, Korean
and Japanese lines typed, converted and turned came out as typed in 224
updates with no refusal.

